PyDoc_STRVAR(__pyx_doc_6pandas_5_libs_6tslibs_10timedeltas_10_Timedelta_22isoformat, "\n        Format the Timedelta as ISO 8601 Duration.\n\n        ``P[n]Y[n]M[n]DT[n]H[n]M[n]S``, where the ``[n]`` s are replaced by the\n        values. See Wikipedia:\n        `ISO 8601 \302\247 Durations <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601#Durations>`_.\n\n        Returns\n        -------\n        str\n\n        See Also\n        --------\n        Timestamp.isoformat : Function is used to convert the given\n            Timestamp object into the ISO format.\n\n        Notes\n        -----\n        The longest component is days, whose value may be larger than\n        365.\n        Every component is always included, even if its value is 0.\n        Pandas uses nanosecond precision, so up to 9 decimal places may\n        be included in the seconds component.\n        Trailing 0\047s are removed from the seconds component after the decimal.\n        We do not 0 pad components, so it\047s `...T5H...`, not `...T05H...`\n\n        Examples\n        --------\n        >>> td = pd.Timedelta(days=6, minutes=50, seconds=3,\n        ...                   milliseconds=10, microseconds=10, nanoseconds=12)\n\n        >>> td.isoformat()\n        \047P6DT0H50M3.010010012S\047\n        >>> pd.Timedelta(hours=1, seconds=10).isoformat()\n        \047P0DT1H0M10S\047\n        >>> pd.Timedelta(days=500.5).isoformat()\n        \047P500DT12H0M0S\047\n        ");
